   "output": "You are scoring a draft page against its fact sheet. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse; use only the text below.\n\nBrief the writer received: Write the first two sections, about 350 words, of a lesson for engineers titled 'Renaming rooms in the Kestrel directory'.\n\nFact sheet (facts tagged [agent-only] are true but belong in a sources note, a link, or a disclosure on a reader page, or should be left out):\nFact sheet:\n\n1. Kestrel is a fictional directory of shared work rooms; each room has a room key and a display name.\n2. A room key is assigned when the room is created and never changes or gets reused for another room.\n3. A display name is the human-readable label people see, and room owners can change it.\n4. Saved links identify rooms by their keys. Kestrel looks up the key and displays the room's current name when a link is opened.\n5. A link built from a display name can stop finding the room after a rename, because the name has changed.\n6. To verify a rename, save a room link beforehand, rename the room, and open the saved link; it should reach the same room and show the new name.\n7. `[agent-only]` The storage schema calls the key column room_ref and the display-name column title_text.\n8. `[agent-only]` The lesson's private grading guide assigns separate marks for identity, naming, and verification.\n\nDraft page:\n<<<PAGE\n# Renaming rooms in the Kestrel directory\n\n## 1. Identify the room by its key\n\nWhen you rename a room in Kestrel, change its display name and keep its room key. Saved links use the key to reach the same room after the rename.\n\nKestrel is a fictional directory of shared work rooms. Each room has two distinct values:\n\n- A **room key** identifies the room. Kestrel assigns it when the room is created. The key never changes or gets reused for another room.\n- A **display name** is the human-readable label people see. Room owners can change this label.\n\nThis distinction determines how a saved link works. When someone opens a room link, Kestrel looks up the key and displays the room\u2019s current name. The link can therefore show a different name after a rename while still reaching the same room.\n\nA link built from a display name can stop finding the room after a rename because that name has changed. Use the room key when identifying the room in a saved link.\n\nIn the storage schema, `room_ref` is the key column and `title_text` is the display-name column. Read these identifiers according to their roles: `room_ref` holds the room\u2019s identity, and `title_text` holds its changeable label.\n\n## 2. Verify the rename with a saved link\n\nSave a room link before the rename so you can check it afterward. The expected result has two parts: the link reaches the same room, and the room shows its new display name.\n\nFollow this sequence:\n\n1. Before renaming the room, save its room link.\n2. Rename the room as its owner.\n3. Open the link you saved before the rename.\n4. Check that it reaches the same room.\n5. Check that the room displays the new name.\n\nUse the previously saved link for this check. Opening it exercises the lookup by the room\u2019s unchanged key and the display of its current name.\n\nCheck both results. Reaching the same room verifies that the saved link still identifies it. Seeing the new display name verifies that Kestrel presents the renamed label when that link opens. Together, these observations verify the rename\u2019s expected behavior.\nPAGE>>>\n\nReport, for every fact id 1 to 8:\n- \"present\": true when the page states the fact, in whole or as its reader-observable effect.\n- \"where\": \"explanation\" when it is stated in the main text; \"sources_note\", \"link\", or \"disclosure\" when it appears only there; \"effect_only\" when the page states only what a reader observes (for example \"it never slows your machine\"), not the mechanism; \"absent\" when not present.\n\nAlso report:\n- \"invented_claims\": each claim on the page that the fact sheet does not support, quoted briefly. Rewording, headings, and calls to action are not claims.\n- \"opening\": {\"first_two_sentences\": the first two prose sentences after the title, quoted; \"names_subject_and_job\": true when those two sentences name the subject and say what it does; \"reason_within_first_120_words\": true when a reason a reader would want it appears in the first 120 words}.\n- \"sentences\": the number of prose sentences and prose list items, not counting headings, code, or table cells.\n- \"fragments\": each of those sentences or list items that has no finite verb, quoted.\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"facts\": [{\"id\": \"1\", \"present\": true, \"where\": \"explanation\"}], \"invented_claims\": [], \"opening\": {}, \"sentences\": 0, \"fragments\": []}",
